\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

Cross-family results (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy}) use seed$=$42 data across six models from five independent families. Within-family IRT analyses (\S\ref{sec:calibration_results}--\ref{sec:domain_differential}) use $J=16$ Qwen2.5 models (8$\times$1.5B + 8$\times$3B); domain-level contamination estimates $\gamma_d$ (\S\ref{sec:domain_differential}) are reported at $J=16$ in the text and $J=13$ in Figure~\ref{fig:domain_gamma}.

\subsection{Dose-Response Patterns}
\label{sec:taxonomy}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Observed dose-response patterns under controlled SFT injection (seed$=$42, MMLU). $\Delta$acc: d000$\to$d100 change (95\% CI from item-level paired bootstrap, $B=10{,}000$). Stab.: bootstrap classification stability. Classifications are robust to threshold hyperparameter choice (5/5 original families at 100\% stability across 30 reasonable $(\tau,\sigma)$ combinations; Appendix~\ref{sec:classification_sensitivity}).}
\label{tab:taxonomy}
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{llccccc}
\toprule
Pattern & Model & Baseline & $\Delta$acc & 95\% CI & Peak & Stab. \\
\midrule
Immune & Phi-4 14.7B & 76.4\% & $\pm$0.5pp & [$-$0.60, $+$0.26] & negl. & 100\% \\
Collapse & Qwen-14B & 69.4\% & $-$9.4 to $-$12.7pp & [$-$12.74, $-$9.0] & --- & 100\% \\
V-recovery & Qwen-7B & 66.1\% & $-$1.8pp & [$-$2.36, $-$1.22] & $-$3.1\textsuperscript{d025} & 99.6\% \\
Benefit & Gemma-2 9B & 61.9\% & $+$3.8pp & [$+$3.18, $+$4.33] & mono. & 76.6\% \\
Benefit & Falcon3 7B & 58.8\% & $+$4.2pp & [$+$3.60, $+$4.79] & mono. & 96.1\% \\
Benefit & OLMo-2 7B & 53.8\% & $+$3.2pp\textsuperscript{$\dagger$} & [$+$2.73, $+$3.71] & sat.\textsuperscript{d050} & 100\% \\
\bottomrule
\multicolumn{7}{l}{\scriptsize $^{\dagger}$Seed-sensitive: s42 $+$3.2pp, s123 $-$0.6pp, s0 $+$1.4pp; cross-seed CI crosses zero. See \S\ref{sec:cross_seed}.}
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Applying Algorithm~\ref{alg:profile-classification} to seed$=$42 accuracy trajectories yields a spectrum of dose-response patterns (Table~\ref{tab:taxonomy}, Figure~\ref{fig:dose_response}), not a uniform inflation effect. These empirical profiles are preliminary at $N\!=\!6$ families.

\paragraph{Baseline ability gradient.}
In our six-family sample, contamination vulnerability concentrates in the 65--70\% MMLU baseline band (Table~\ref{tab:taxonomy}). Below 62\%, three Benefit families gain $+$3.2 to $+$4.2pp; in the vulnerable band, Qwen-7B shows V-shaped harm-then-recovery while Qwen-14B collapses ($-$9.4 to $-$25.0pp across seeds)---3$\times$ more fragile despite higher baseline. Above 76\%, Phi-4 is immune. ARC direction is consistent for 3/5 families (Appendix~\ref{sec:arc_cross_benchmark}); STEM is the most resilient domain, Social Sciences the most sensitive at 1.8$\times$ STEM magnitude (Appendix~\ref{sec:domain_decomposition}), foreshadowing \S\ref{sec:domain_differential}.

\paragraph{Out-of-sample validation.}
Out-of-sample validation on LLaMA-3.1-8B (Meta; d000, d025, d100) yields Immune on both MMLU ($-$0.69pp) and ARC ($+$0.09pp), despite matching OLMo-2's Benefit baseline (53.5\% vs.\ 53.8\%)---demonstrating family-level factors beyond baseline ability modulate vulnerability. Two of four patterns replicate across families: Immune (Phi-4, LLaMA-3.1), Benefit (OLMo-2, Falcon3, Gemma-2). LLaMA-3.1 shows the strongest GSM8K transfer ($+$16.4pp; Appendix~\ref{sec:gsm8k_dose_response}), demonstrating target-benchmark immunity does not imply transfer immunity.

\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Transfer}
\label{sec:gsm8k_transfer}

Contamination patterns are a property of the (model, benchmark) pair, not the model alone. Within MCQ format, MMLU--ARC direction agreement is 86\% (6/7 families; Appendix~\ref{sec:arc_cross_benchmark}); across formats (MCQ$\to$generative math), agreement drops to 40\% (2/5) with three families exhibiting directional reversal on GSM8K \citep{cobbe2021gsm8k} (Table~\ref{tab:gsm8k_transfer}, Appendix). All five non-excluded families show positive GSM8K transfer ($+$1 to $+$16pp), including Qwen-14B ($-$9.4pp MMLU yet $+$13.1pp GSM8K), suggesting capability elicitation rather than memorization, consistent with null DiD (\S\ref{sec:memorization_test}). Volume effects are ruled out: d005 ($+$1.5\% data) yields $+$11.1pp for Qwen-7B, while Phi-4 shows near-zero transfer.

\subsection{Cross-Seed Stability}
\label{sec:cross_seed}

With $n\!=\!3$ seeds per family, cross-seed CIs have limited precision (widths 1.5--14.5pp; Table~\ref{tab:cross_seed}, Appendix). Under our aggregation rule (\S\ref{sec:classification}), 4/5 families receive consistent direction-level classifications; direction is more reproducible than shape (strict sign consistency 2/5). OLMo-2's CI crosses zero, rendering its classification borderline. Conflicting contamination findings in the literature may partly reflect unreported seed variance.

\subsection{Item-Level Memorization Test}
\label{sec:memorization_test}

We test whether dose-response patterns arise from item-specific memorization using the treated/untreated split at d025 (3{,}540 / 10{,}502 MMLU items). No model shows significant DiD ($p > 0.05$; Table~\ref{tab:did}), ruling out item-specific effects $>$2pp (MDES 1.25--2.05pp; ARC MDES in Appendix~\ref{sec:mdes}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Treated vs.\ untreated item accuracy changes at d025 MMLU (3{,}540 / 10{,}502). All DiD non-significant. $^{**}p<.01$; $^{***}p<.001$; ns: $p>.05$.}
\label{tab:did}
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Model & Treated $\Delta$ & Untreated $\Delta$ & DiD & Verdict \\
\midrule
Gemma-2 9B & $+$3.1$^{**}$ & $+$2.6$^{***}$ & $+$0.5 (ns) & generalization \\
OLMo-2 7B & $+$1.6 (ns) & $+$2.7$^{***}$ & $-$1.1 (ns) & mixed \\
Phi-4 14.7B & $+$0.3 (ns) & $+$0.6 (ns) & $-$0.3 (ns) & no-effect \\
Qwen-14B & $-$6.9$^{***}$ & $-$7.6$^{***}$ & $+$0.7 (ns) & anti-contam. \\
Qwen-7B & $-$3.8$^{**}$ & $-$2.9$^{***}$ & $-$0.8 (ns) & anti-contam. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Parallel changes suggest global capability modulation rather than memorization (Figure~\ref{fig:treated_untreated}, Appendix); ARC's limited power precludes this conclusion there.

\subsection{Calibration Quality}
\label{sec:calibration_results}

Within-family IRT analysis uses Qwen2.5 $\leq$3B ($J=16$), where aggregate contamination shifts accuracy by ${\sim}$0.6--0.7pp. RRF-guided item filtering achieves $\tau = 0.73$ on 300 items ($\Delta\tau = 0.18$ vs.\ size-matched random; signal ablation in Appendix~\ref{sec:rrf_ablation}). IRT is fitted on these 300 highest-exposure-variance items (2.1\% of MMLU); contamination effects on remaining low-variance items are not empirically verified.

Table~\ref{tab:baselines} compares methods on 300 RRF-filtered items. Raw accuracy's MAE advantage (0.006 vs.\ ContamCal's 0.009) is predicted by Eq.~\eqref{eq:calibrated}: calibrated scores depend on $\theta_j$, not $\gamma_d$ directly; under near-null aggregate contamination, both estimators converge. ContamCal's value lies in the diagnostic pathway ($\gamma_d$), not aggregate calibration. B6-DCR systematically overcorrects (MAE 0.108). Domain analysis reveals Social Sciences $\gamma_d = -0.63$ ($P(\gamma_d\!<\!0) = 99.7\%$; \S\ref{sec:domain_differential}), while pooled $\gamma$ remains non-significant---consistent with opposing domain-level effects canceling. Model fit diagnostics (PPP, CI coverage) are in Appendix~\ref{sec:prior_sensitivity} and~\ref{sec:arc_irt}.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{MMLU calibration on 300 RRF-filtered items (Qwen2.5, $J$=16). ARC results in Appendix~\ref{sec:arc_irt}.}
\label{tab:baselines}
\footnotesize
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Method & MAE & $\tau$ \\
\midrule
B0: Raw Accuracy & \textbf{0.006} & 0.767 \\
B1b: Remove \& Rescore & 0.026 & \underline{0.783} \\
B3: Vanilla 2PL & 0.008 & 0.731 \\
B4: Linear Regression & 0.010 & 0.751 \\
B5b: Emperor & \textbf{0.006} & \textbf{0.789} \\
B6: DCR (reimpl.) & 0.108 & 0.654 \\
ContamCal (2PL+$\gamma$) & 0.009 & 0.731 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Detection Signal Failure Modes}
\label{sec:signal_failure}

Min-K\%++ \citep{zhang_2024_minkpp} exhibits \textit{directional reversal} at 0.5B: contaminated items receive \textit{lower} scores (paired Cohen's $d = -1.98$ at d100, $|d| > 1.4$ at d005). S1 achieves AUC $> 0.95$ at all scales; S4 is near-random below 1.5B (Appendix~\ref{sec:signal_reliability_appendix}). Simulation shows $\gamma$ recovery requires AUC$\geq$0.55 with $J \geq 50$; at AUC $\geq 0.90$, $J = 16$ suffices (Appendix~\ref{sec:identifiability_appendix}).

\subsection{Domain-Level Analysis}
\label{sec:domain_differential}

Within-family domain analysis (Qwen2.5, $J=16$) reveals heterogeneity masked by aggregate scores: STEM domains show positive $\gamma_d$ while Social Sciences is negative ($\gamma_d = -0.63$, 95\% CI $[-1.07, -0.19]$, $P(\gamma_d\!<\!0) = 99.7\%$, PPP$=$0.722; Figure~\ref{fig:domain_gamma}, Table~\ref{tab:domain_gamma} in Appendix). Social Sciences $P(\gamma_d\!<\!0)$ is 99.35--99.85\% across priors (Table~\ref{tab:socsci_prior}), exceeding the Bonferroni threshold ($99.375\%$) under baseline and loose priors but falling 0.03\,pp short under the tight prior. The directional signal is robust; we treat domain-level findings as exploratory pending replication with larger ensembles.

