Applying Algorithm~\ref{alg:profile-classification} to seed$=$42 accuracy trajectories yields a spectrum of dose-response patterns (Table~\ref{tab:taxonomy}, Figure~\ref{fig:dose_response}), not a uniform inflation effect. These empirical profiles are preliminary at $N\!=\!6$ families.

\paragraph{Baseline ability gradient.}
In our six-family sample, contamination vulnerability concentrates in the 65--70\% MMLU baseline band (Table~\ref{tab:taxonomy}). Below 62\%, three Benefit families gain $+$3.2 to $+$4.2pp; in the vulnerable band, Qwen-7B shows V-shaped harm-then-recovery while Qwen-14B collapses ($-$9.4 to $-$25.0pp across seeds)---3$\times$ more fragile despite higher baseline. Above 76\%, Phi-4 is immune. ARC direction is consistent for 3/5 families (Appendix~\ref{sec:arc_cross_benchmark}); STEM is the most resilient domain, Social Sciences the most sensitive at 1.8$\times$ STEM magnitude (Appendix~\ref{sec:domain_decomposition}), foreshadowing \S\ref{sec:domain_differential}.

\paragraph{Out-of-sample validation.}
Out-of-sample validation on LLaMA-3.1-8B (Meta; d000, d025, d100) yields Immune on both MMLU ($-$0.69pp) and ARC ($+$0.09pp), despite matching OLMo-2's Benefit baseline (53.5\% vs.\ 53.8\%)---demonstrating family-level factors beyond baseline ability modulate vulnerability. Two of four patterns replicate across families: Immune (Phi-4, LLaMA-3.1), Benefit (OLMo-2, Falcon3, Gemma-2). LLaMA-3.1 shows the strongest GSM8K transfer ($+$16.4pp; Appendix~\ref{sec:gsm8k_dose_response}), demonstrating target-benchmark immunity does not imply transfer immunity.

\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Transfer}
\label{sec:gsm8k_transfer}

Contamination patterns are a property of the (model, benchmark) pair, not the model alone. Within MCQ format, MMLU--ARC direction agreement is 86\% (6/7 families; Appendix~\ref{sec:arc_cross_benchmark}); across formats (MCQ$\to$generative math), agreement drops to 40\% (2/5) with three families exhibiting directional reversal on GSM8K \citep{cobbe2021gsm8k} (Table~\ref{tab:gsm8k_transfer}, Appendix). All five non-excluded families show positive GSM8K transfer ($+$1 to $+$16pp), including Qwen-14B ($-$9.4pp MMLU yet $+$13.1pp GSM8K), suggesting capability elicitation rather than memorization, consistent with null DiD (\S\ref{sec:memorization_test}). Volume effects are ruled out: d005 ($+$1.5\% data) yields $+$11.1pp for Qwen-7B, while Phi-4 shows near-zero transfer.

\subsection{Cross-Seed Stability}
\label{sec:cross_seed}

With $n\!=\!3$ seeds per family, cross-seed CIs have limited precision (widths 1.5--14.5pp; Table~\ref{tab:cross_seed}, Appendix). Under our aggregation rule (\S\ref{sec:classification}), 4/5 families receive consistent direction-level classifications; direction is more reproducible than shape (strict sign consistency 2/5). OLMo-2's CI crosses zero, rendering its classification borderline. Conflicting contamination findings in the literature may partly reflect unreported seed variance.

\subsection{Item-Level Memorization Test}
\label{sec:memorization_test}

We test whether dose-response patterns arise from item-specific memorization using the treated/untreated split at d025 (3{,}540 / 10{,}502 MMLU items). No model shows significant DiD ($p > 0.05$; Table~\ref{tab:did}), ruling out item-specific effects $>$2pp (MDES 1.25--2.05pp; ARC MDES in Appendix~\ref{sec:mdes}).

